set up mode local in 7 steps:
  1. check that the key edit applies to /Users/user_name/Library/LaunchAgents/local.litellm.proxy.plist, then back it up to /Users/user_name/.config/wt-model/backups/local.litellm.proxy.plist.bak-<date>-<time> (a 0700 directory, outside LaunchAgents so launchd never loads the copy; its mode without group or other bits; it keeps the old key)
  2. replace LITELLM_MASTER_KEY in /Users/user_name/Library/LaunchAgents/local.litellm.proxy.plist with a new generated key (not shown), keeping every other byte of the file; it is written without group or other bits
  3. set wt-model's LiteLLM pair in /Users/user_name/.config/wt-model/: url http://localhost:4000, key the new LITELLM_MASTER_KEY (not shown)
  4. restart LiteLLM so it uses the new key: unload it (launchctl bootout gui/501/local.litellm.proxy) and load the edited plist (launchctl bootstrap gui/501 /Users/user_name/Library/LaunchAgents/local.litellm.proxy.plist); a kickstart -k would restart the definition launchd already holds, with the old key
  5. wait for LiteLLM to answer at http://localhost:4000/health/liveliness and accept the new master key
  6. restart the LaunchAgent so it reads the new pair: launchctl kickstart -k gui/501/local.wt-model.serve
  7. wait for wt-model to answer at http://127.0.0.1:8787
left alone and notes:
  - keep the token file /Users/user_name/.config/wt-model/server.token
  - keep the LaunchAgent plist /Users/user_name/Library/LaunchAgents/local.wt-model.serve.plist (unchanged)
  - keep [model-server] in /Users/user_name/.config/agent-wt/config.toml (already mode = "local")
  - anything else that holds the old master key stops working once LiteLLM restarts: other clients and scripts that send it, a [litellm] api_key in /Users/user_name/.config/agent-wt/config.toml if one is set there, and copies of the plist (the backup keeps the old key; remove it once the new key works)
  - never changed in /Users/user_name/Library/LaunchAgents/local.litellm.proxy.plist: LITELLM_SALT_KEY, DATABASE_URL, provider keys and the UI login
  - a restart or unload here (launchctl kickstart -k, bootout) ends the requests in flight at once; unlike `wt-model litellm restart`, nothing waits for them to finish
